* ============================================================================
* validate_unique_repair_cnx_proof.dts
*
* Task #33. Does VALIDATE UNIQUE ... REPAIR maintain a CNX order?
*
* WHY THIS EXISTS
*   VUREPAIR (validate_unique_repair_index_proof.dts) proved REPAIR maintains
*   the active index on x64/CDX, and its header says in as many words:
*
*       MUST run on x64/CDX, not x32/CNX. CnxBackend::upsert/erase are still
*       stubs (lane XIDX-TXN-02), so a CNX table cannot maintain on mutation
*       at all and this proof would fail there for an unrelated reason.
*
*   That restriction LAPSED on 2026-07-31 when XIDX-TXN-02 M1 gave CnxBackend
*   working upsert/erase. The blocker is gone. Whether REPAIR actually maintains
*   a CNX order was never measured -- the restriction was simply left in place.
*   This script measures it.
*
*   The claim under test is not about CNX in isolation. It is that REPAIR routes
*   through DbArea::replaceFieldStored() and therefore through the index replace
*   snapshot, which is BACKEND-AGNOSTIC. If that is true, REPAIR inherits CNX
*   maintenance for free and this proof passes with no source change. If it does
*   not pass, REPAIR has a CDX-shaped assumption in it and the open index API
*   has another instance of the defect it exists to remove.
*
* WHAT THIS TESTS THAT THE CDX ORIGINAL COULD NOT
*   VUREPAIR asserts through SEEK only. On CDX that is sufficient, because a
*   CDX tag stores keys and a seek is a key lookup.
*
*   A CNX RUN1 payload stores 4 bytes per recno and NO KEYS, so SEEK and ORDER
*   are answered by different machinery, and IDXSTALE proved they can DISAGREE:
*   a seek compares live field values through the recno ordering, so it can
*   succeed over an ordering that is wrong. Asserting only through SEEK on CNX
*   would therefore be a weaker test than the CDX one, not an equivalent one.
*
*   VURC_T3/T4 close that: they score ORDERED POSITION, which is the property
*   M1 and M2 actually deliver, and T4 requires the maintained order to survive
*   a REBUILD unchanged.
*
* FIXTURE AND EXPECTED ARITHMETIC
*   SID 10, 20, 20, 30. Rec 3 is the duplicate REPAIR must renumber, and
*   compute_max_numeric_value() sees 30, so rec 3 becomes 31 -- deterministic,
*   same as the CDX proof.
*
*     before repair, ascending:  10 ADAMS  20 MILLER  20 ZEBRA  30 YOUNG
*     after  repair, ascending:  10 ADAMS  20 MILLER  30 YOUNG  31 ZEBRA
*
*   So ZEBRA moves from the middle of the equal-key run to the BOTTOM. That
*   movement is the observable, and it is why the guard checks TOP before and
*   the assertions check BOTTOM after.
*
* MARKER DESIGN
*   Every marker is a FIELD comparison, for the reason VUREPAIR learned the hard
*   way and CNXLIVE/IDXSTALE/CNXBUF/CNXPERSIST inherited: RECNO() and FOUND()
*   render EMPTY in a "?" marker and STR() does not rescue them. Park the cursor
*   on a known different record before any seek, so a stale cursor cannot fake a
*   pass -- VUREPAIR did not park, and this one does.
*
* KNOWN RISK, stated so a failure can be read
*   The CNX proofs to date (CNXLIVE, CNXBUF, CNXPERSIST) all index a CHARACTER
*   field. This one indexes a NUMERIC field, because the uniqueness candidate is
*   SID. If T1/T2 fail while T3/T4 pass, suspect numeric SEEK on a keyless
*   payload rather than REPAIR -- that is a different defect, in a different
*   lane, and it should not be reported as a REPAIR failure.
*
* Self-contained: builds and erases its own throwaway v32 table; never touches
* STUDENTS or any live fixture.
*   Standalone:  DOTSCRIPT scripts/validate_unique_repair_cnx_proof.dts
*   Capture:     SET ALTERNATE TO <file> / SET ALTERNATE ON  (never DOTSCRIPT OUT)
*
* Lane record: XIDX_TXN_02_PDLC_LANE_V1.md
* Runtime status: NOT YET RUN. Authored 2026-08-01.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* x32 lane: CNX is the v32 indexing backend
DO x32

FORMULA "VU-REPAIR-CNX-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- throwaway v32 table with a duplicated key value -------------------------
ERASE TABLE VUREPCNX CONFIRM
CREATE VUREPCNX (SID N(6), LNAME C(20))

APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 10
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ADAMS"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 20
REPLACE LNAME WITH "MILLER"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 20
REPLACE LNAME WITH "ZEBRA"
APPEND
REPLACE SID WITH 30
REPLACE LNAME WITH "YOUNG"

* --- index the uniqueness-candidate field and make it the active order -------
CNX CREATE
CNX ADDTAG SID
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG SID

* --- VURC_G0/G1: fixture guards ----------------------------------------------
* If the order is not right BEFORE the repair, nothing below means anything.
GO TOP
? "VURC_G0_baseline_top_is_ADAMS:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ADAMS")

BOTTOM
? "VURC_G1_baseline_bottom_is_YOUNG:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "YOUNG")

* ============================================================================
* THE REPAIR. No REINDEX and no REBUILD after this line until T4, deliberately.
* ============================================================================
VALIDATE UNIQUE FIELD SID REPAIR

* --- VURC_D1/D2: DIAGNOSTIC -- what did REPAIR actually write? ---------------
* Added after the first run (2026-08-01) came back T1/T2 green and T3/T4 red,
* which left two candidate causes and no way to tell them apart:
*
*   D1 .T. -> rec 3 holds 31, so REPAIR wrote the value we expect and any
*             ordering failure is a MAINTENANCE failure.
*   D1 .F. -> REPAIR wrote something else, T1's "Found at 3" was a soft landing
*             rather than an exact hit, and the ordering is arguably CORRECT for
*             the value actually stored. Different defect entirely.
*
* Read by direct record access, not through the order, so a wrong ordering
* cannot colour the answer. STR() on a numeric renders inside a marker where
* the bare numeric comparison would not -- same rule as everywhere else.
GO 3
? "VURC_D1_rec3_sid_is_31:" + (ALLTRIM(STR(SID)) = "31")
? "VURC_D2_rec3_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- VURC_T1: the repaired record is reachable at its NEW key ----------------
* Mirrors VUR_T2 on the CDX side. Park on a known different record first so the
* assertion cannot pass on a cursor that never moved.
GO 1
SEEK 31
? "VURC_T1_seek_new_key_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- VURC_T2: the surviving legitimate 20 is still MILLER --------------------
* Guards the other direction: the repair must not have removed the wrong entry
* from the equal-key run. Mirrors VUR_T3.
GO 1
SEEK 20
? "VURC_T2_seek_old_key_is_MILLER:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "MILLER")

* --- VURC_T3: THE CNX-SPECIFIC ASSERTION -- the ORDER absorbed the repair ----
* This is the one the CDX proof could not make. 31 is now the largest SID, so
* ZEBRA must be at the BOTTOM of the ordering, with no rebuild anywhere between
* the repair and this read. If REPAIR wrote the record but not the order, T1 can
* still pass (a seek compares live values) while this reads YOUNG.
* RE-SCORED 2026-08-01 after the walk below. This assertion used BOTTOM and
* read .F., which looked like a maintenance failure and was not: the walk shows
* the ordering IS 1,2,4,3 with ZEBRA last. BOTTOM is what disagrees with it.
* Walk the order from TOP instead, so the marker scores the ORDERING rather
* than one navigation verb's opinion of it.
GO TOP
SKIP
SKIP
SKIP
? "VURC_T3_order_absorbed_repair_last_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- VURC_F1: the BOTTOM disagreement, kept as its own marker ----------------
* EXPECTED .F. UNTIL FIXED. Not a failure of this lane -- it is the finding
* this proof turned up, and deleting the assertion would lose it. BOTTOM must
* land on the last entry of the active order, which the walk shows is record 3.
*
* It is NOT simply "BOTTOM ignores the order": CNXBUF B4 and CNXPERSIST P4 both
* assert bottom-is-ZEBRA on a CHARACTER tag where ordered-last and physical-last
* differ, and both pass. So something diverges between the two paths, and this
* marker is the standing evidence until someone measures which.
BOTTOM
? "VURC_F1_BOTTOM_agrees_with_order_EXPECT_F:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

* --- DIAGNOSTIC WALK -- read the ordering, do not infer it -------------------
* Added after the second run (2026-08-01). T3 and T4 both read .F., with T4
* AFTER a rebuild that reads the table and sorts it. The table holds
* 10/20/31/30 on records 1/2/3/4, so ascending order must end at record 3 --
* and BOTTOM reports record 4 under BOTH the maintained and the rebuilt order.
*
* No ordering rule I can construct puts 30 after 31: not numeric, and not
* string either, since "31" > "30" on a fixed-width right-aligned N(6) field.
* TOP and BOTTOM are two samples of a sequence; that is not enough to diagnose
* a sequence. Print the whole thing.
*
* This is NOT scored. It exists so the next reader sees the permutation instead
* of a deduction about it -- two wrong deductions have already been made about
* this exact test.
FORMULA "VURC_WALK_AFTER_REPAIR:"
CNX WALK SID
SMARTLIST 10

* --- VURC_T4: and the maintained order AGREES WITH A REBUILD -----------------
* Same shape as CNXLIVE L_T6, CNXBUF B5 and CNXPERSIST P6. If the maintained
* permutation differs from what the table implies, the order CHANGES across this
* line and T3 was measuring a consistent lie. The rebuild must be a no-op.
REBUILD
SET ORDER TAG SID

* RE-SCORED for the same reason as T3. The rebuilt ordering is 1,2,4,3 -- the
* walk below prints it -- so a rebuild DOES agree with the maintained order,
* which is the property this marker exists to assert.
GO TOP
SKIP
SKIP
SKIP
? "VURC_T4_rebuild_agrees_last_is_ZEBRA:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ZEBRA")

GO TOP
? "VURC_T5_rebuild_agrees_top_is_ADAMS:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ADAMS")

* --- DIAGNOSTIC WALK, post-rebuild -------------------------------------------
* The discriminating one. A rebuild reads the TABLE and sorts it, so this
* permutation is what collect_sorted_recnos_for_tag_ believes ascending order
* to be for a numeric tag. If it differs from 1,2,4,3 the defect is in the
* rebuild sort and has nothing to do with realtime maintenance or with M2 --
* which would also mean CNX numeric ordering has been wrong all along and no
* fixture caught it, because every existing fixture already ascends by recno.
FORMULA "VURC_WALK_AFTER_REBUILD:"
CNX WALK SID
SMARTLIST 10

FORMULA "VU-REPAIR-CNX-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown ----------------------------------------------------------------
* No bare "SET ORDER TO": it reports "SET ORDER: missing target."
* CLOSE drops the order with the table -- and, since XIDX-TXN-02 M2, also
* persists any maintained ordering, so expect a [CNX SAVE] line here.
CLOSE
ERASE TABLE VUREPCNX CONFIRM
